  "text_md": "EXTREMAL ELASTICITY OF QUADRATIC ORDERS 15\n\nBy (13), we have\n\nlog xi log log xi\n\n2 > exp (log 2) exp C\n\ngi ≥\n\n![](<2503.07801_pg15_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\np inert in K (p+1)|Mxi\n\n> i\n\nfor suﬃciently large i. Moreover, we have L(gi) | Mx\n\n, which implies that L(gi) ≤ Mx\n\ni\n\ni ≤ x2i = (log i)(4/C) log log logi < (log gi)c\n\n0 log log log gi\n\nfor suﬃciently large i, where c0 := 4/C. The asserted upper bound follows by extracting a strictly monotonic subsequence {fi}i≥1 from {gi}i≥1.\n\n5. Minimal order in the real case: Proof of Theorem 1.4\n\nLet K be a real quadratic ﬁeld. We remind the reader that ǫ denotes the fundamental unit of K, and we set\n\n- 1 if NK/Q(ǫ) = 1,\n- 2 if NK/Q(ǫ) = −1.\n\n\nδ =\n\nIf p is a prime inert in K, then its associated Frobenius element is conjugation on K (the nontrivial element of Gal(K/Q)). Hence,\n\nǫp+1 ≡ ǫpǫ ≡ NK/Q(ǫ) (mod pOK), and\n\nǫδ(p+1) ≡ NK/Q(ǫ)δ ≡ 1 (mod pOK).\n\nThus, the order of ǫ in (OK/pOK)× is a divisor of δ(p + 1). We will base our proof of Theorem 1.4 on the following result of Roskam [Ros00].\n\n- Proposition 5.1 (conditional on GRH). There are inﬁnitely many primes p, inert in K, for which the order of ǫ in (OK/pOK)× is precisely δ(p + 1).\n\n(In fact, Roskam shows that the order is δ(p+1) not only for inﬁnitely many inert primes, but for a positive proportion of all inert primes. The weaker version here is suﬃcient for our purposes.) Theorem 1.4 is an immediate consequence of Proposition 5.1 in conjunction with the next assertion.\n\n- Proposition 5.2. If p is a prime inert in K for which ǫ has order δ(p + 1) in (OK/pOK)×, then\n\n\n3 2\n\nρ(Op) ≤ hK +\n\n.\n\n![](<2503.07801_pg15_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nProof. Let p be as in the proposition. Then ǫℓ(p) ≡ n (mod pOK) for some rational integer n prime to p. By Fermat’s little theorem,\n\nǫ(p−1)ℓ(p) ≡ 1 (mod pOK). We are assuming that ǫ has order δ(p+1) in (OK/pOK)×. Hence, the displayed congruence forces p + 1 | δ(p + 1) | (p − 1)ℓ(p).\n\nWriting (p − 1)ℓ(p) = (p + 1)ℓ(p) − 2ℓ(p), we deduce that p + 1 | 2ℓ(p). In particular, ℓ(p) ≥\n\np + 1 2\n\n.\n\n![](<2503.07801_pg15_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\n",
